{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for the bounded thread-local event railway at exactly 43b0c49af2f3cfdda0d8c3e46bc031a3db55c637.\n\nAll three findings of my earlier changes-requested review (c4fd4e4c) are closed at this head, and I checked each by measurement rather than by reading. The gates pass and the embedded bundle is deterministic. What stops me approving is not the code: while this branch was in review the same feature landed on main from a parallel branch, so this head can no longer be integrated, and the condition it was built to satisfy — \"integrate onto exact current main without losing accepted UI work\" — is no longer met.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED, AND HOW\n\nCheckout. /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/ui-thread-event-railway-review-repair is clean (empty git status --porcelain --untracked-files=normal), HEAD is exactly 43b0c49af2f3cfdda0d8c3e46bc031a3db55c637, and git diff --check is clean. I re-read HEAD at the start and at the end of the review and it did not move. All three implementing commits carry Rests-On: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59fbf34f78f92ef07e59a963b3d58eaa1cc1e1d4 (9bc0b2e \"Bound the event thread railway\", a345051 \"Integrate current main into the event railway repair\", 43b0c49 \"Regenerate the integrated workroom bundle\"). I did not modify that worktree. Every experiment ran in two detached scratch worktrees at the same head, each pinned with git rev-parse HEAD in the same command as the gate it ran, and left clean.\n\nGates, in a scratch worktree pinned at 43b0c49. make vet clean. make test: all 15 packages ok. make race: all 15 packages ok (kernel 129s). cd ui \u0026\u0026 npm ci \u0026\u0026 npm test: 47 of 47 pass. make ui-check run twice in succession: both rebuilds leave git status --porcelain -- internal/service/uidist empty, so the committed bundle (index-5vTn7N7q.css, index-C5K22sSD.js) is reproduced byte-for-byte from source at this head. HEAD was identical before and after every run.\n\nFINDING 1 OF c4fd4e4c (unbounded lanes) — CLOSED, MEASURED\n\nI re-ran the live-log geometry probe: the shipped src/lib/threads.ts, src/lib/threadRailway.ts and src/components/ThreadRailway.tsx loaded through Vite SSR over this repository's own /v0/status, now 1,614 durable events, 980 threads with replies, 25,045 rows.\n\nAt this head: 25,045 rows in and 25,045 rows out, no duplicates, so nothing is dropped. Maximum 6 lanes, widest rail graph 128px. Zero threads wider than the 360px desktop content box; zero wider than the 296px phone box; zero leaving under 120px for the label.\n\nFor a like-for-like comparison I checked out only the previous head's layout module (04fa4a38's src/lib/threadRailway.ts) into the same scratch worktree and ran the same probe over the same projection: 527 lanes at worst, a 9,506px rail, 112 threads over the desktop box, 137 over the phone box, 157 under 120px of label. So on identical input the repair takes 112 broken threads to 0 without losing a row.\n\nThe worst thread is still ticket #19, \"Accepted dogfood checkpoint\", 1,309 rows. It previously rendered \u003csvg width=\"8098\" height=\"63104\"\u003e with padding-left: 8102px. At this head it renders \u003csvg width=\"128\" height=\"89012\"\u003e with a maximum padding-left of 132px, 1,309 row elements and 1,309 jump buttons.\n\nBrowser automation. My runtime does have a real browser, unlike the implementer's, so I used it: headless Chrome 151.0.7922.109 rendering the SSR output of the shipped component with the committed stylesheet, in a pane whose width is set inline so the numbers do not depend on which utilities the compiled CSS happens to carry. These are Blink's own boxes, not arithmetic on markup.\n\n  Pane 320px (phone sheet), thread #19, 1,309 rows: rail SVG measures exactly 128 x 89012. Every one of the 1,309 label buttons is 146px wide — none under 120px, none at or below zero. No statement text collapses (narrowest 25.2px, none at zero). No row and no label crosses the pane's right edge.\n  Pane 384px (docked 24rem pane), same thread: labels 210px, and the rail container does not overflow horizontally at all.\n  Threads #29 (256 rows) and #134 (603 rows) measure the same way at 320px.\n\nFolding is visible, not silent. In thread #19, 829 of 1,309 rows sit on the folded lane and 328 fold starts carry the \"folded branch\" marker. Across all 980 threads, every fold start has at least one basis, so the marker can always render (measured: 0 exceptions). I also took painted screenshots at 320px: the folded lane is the rightmost coloured lane and the amber \"folded branch\" text is legible on each branch start.\n\nFINDING 2 OF c4fd4e4c (unpinned conditions) — CLOSED, MEASURED\n\nI reverted each previously unpinned behaviour in a scratch worktree and ran the suite. Baseline 47/47 green. Eleven mutations, eleven caught:\n\n  reverse the railway to newest-first — caught (components test 2)\n  keep the root first but reverse the replies — caught (tests 2 and 16)\n  delete every semantic status marker (ratified, stale, disputed, withdrawn) — caught (test 2)\n  drop only the retired strike-through — caught (test 2)\n  make every row's jump a no-op — caught (test 16)\n  render the railway under both tabs — caught (test 2)\n  drop overflow-x-hidden — caught (tests 2 and 16)\n  raise MAX_THREAD_RAIL_LANES from 6 to 60 — caught (test 14)\n  never reset the view on retarget — caught (test 16)\n  label bases by ticket presence only, losing \"outside thread\" — caught (tests 2 and 15)\n  never release a completed lane, so lanes stop recycling — caught (test 14)\n\nThe worktree was restored to a clean 43b0c49 after each mutation and at the end.\n\nFINDING 3 OF c4fd4e4c (off-thread bases) — CLOSED\n\nrailBasisLabel now takes membership of the laid-out node set rather than ticket presence, and ThreadRailway passes byNode.has(event). The SSR test asserts the rendered \"reply to #1 (outside thread)\" and \"cites #1 (outside thread)\", and reverting the helper fails two tests.\n\nNEW FINDING, BLOCKING — this head is no longer integrable, and the feature is already on main\n\nmain is dfa51178104ea5c2a5c91e81ac72e3f8697b1759. It is not an ancestor of this head; the merge base is 4db6d10, which was main at 14:05. Since then main has taken 41c9fc062cd2cb785d372c9cec4ef694da1dba52, \"Add a thread-local event railway to the Thread panel\", merged at 14:46, which carries three live artifacts at exactly the paths this artifact claims: c31ff753 for ui/src/lib/threadRailway.ts, 2e75f34d for ui/src/components/ThreadRailway.tsx, b64043db for ui/src/components/ThreadPane.tsx. That head was reviewed and approved by codex in report 53ef0af0 at 14:20, five minutes after this artifact was published.\n\nMeasured, not reasoned. A trial merge of main into a detached scratch worktree at this head produces six conflicts, and I aborted it: internal/service/uidist/index.html, ui/src/components/ThreadPane.tsx, ui/src/components/ThreadRailway.tsx, ui/src/lib/threadRailway.ts, ui/test/components.test.mjs, ui/test/interaction.test.mjs. Two of them are add/add — ThreadRailway.tsx and threadRailway.ts were created independently on both sides, so neither version is an ancestor of the other. Main also carries work this head does not: ui/test/dom.test.mjs, a new ui dependency, and changes in cmd/gitseq-mcp, internal/app, internal/service and internal/statusview.\n\nAnd the finding is already closed on main. I ran the same probe with main's merged layout module over the same 980 threads and 25,045 rows: 10 lanes at worst, a 200px rail, zero threads over the desktop or phone box, zero rows lost. So this head is not needed to fix the geometry; it is a second, independent fix for the same defect.\n\nThat makes the first condition of the repair request (59fbf34f) — \"integrate the reviewed event-railway behavior onto exact current main without losing accepted UI work\" — unmet at this head. Approving would put an exact head into step 5 whose merge either conflicts or reverts already-merged, separately approved work, and would retire three live artifacts in favour of an older design. The projection agrees: this artifact reads stale and describes a superseded world.\n\nWhat would close it: a decision about which of the two implementations stands. If main's is kept, supersede this artifact and retire the branch; anything worth keeping from here (the six-lane bound, the \"outside thread\" wording, the mutation set) becomes a small delta cut on top of dfa5117 or later. If this one is preferred, it has to be re-cut on current main as a change to the files that are now there, and reviewed again at that new head.\n\nMINOR, NOT BLOCKING\n\nPhone clipping. At a 320px pane the rail container reports 308px of scroll width inside a 286px client box, and overflow-x-hidden clips the difference. In Blink, 290 inline spans sit up to 10px past the container's right edge: the row's own ticket badge (the \"#29\" in ml-auto shrink-0 font-mono) and long basis lines such as \"reply to #27 (outside thread)\". No label is erased and no row escapes the pane, so the stated condition holds; but the ticket number is partly cut on the narrowest phone and, because the overflow is hidden rather than scrollable, cannot be reached. The screenshots show it. The fix is to let that line shrink rather than to hide it.\n\nFold marking. Folded rows are distinguished by the shared lane and its colour, and only the branch start carries text. A folded continuation row is identifiable only by a data attribute. Main's merged version draws folded rows as grey squares, which reads more plainly.\n\nTest 16 pins source text, not behaviour. \"thread railway wiring pins order, navigation, tab isolation, retarget reset, and narrow bounds\" asserts regexes over the two component files. It did catch the no-op jump and the deleted retarget reset, so those conditions are pinned. But its tab-isolation regex, /view === \"railway\"[\\s\\S]*?data-thread-view-content=\"railway\"/, also matches the tab button's aria-selected={view === \"railway\"} higher in the same file, so on its own it would not notice the render guard going away. The SSR test does notice — I measured that — so the condition is pinned, just not by the test that names it.\n\nWHAT I COULD NOT VERIFY\n\nNo scripted interaction. I had a real browser for layout and painting, but no driver to click, focus or type, so tab switching, the retarget reset and row navigation are verified only through rendered markup, unit tests and the mutation probes — not by pressing anything.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:25cc973bf42ccea8e644360178c5eb228ad832bb","head":"43b0c49af2f3cfdda0d8c3e46bc031a3db55c637","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; describes a superseded world; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8cbb25f8f0679db2419fa0944e9f722c5b8a2a7a","verdict":"changes-requested"}}